/P 196 0 R
/E (AI models require fine-tuning to adjust them for specific contexts, industries, or organisational needs. For instance, a language model trained broadly on text might be fine-tuned to understand medical terminology or legal documents. Optimisation also includes improving efficiency and reducing error rates.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (After training and fine-tuning, the AI system produces outputs, which can include predictions, recommendations, classifications, or generated content. Importantly, AI outputs often feed into other non-AI systems such as business dashboards, workflow software, or customer service platforms. This integration is crucial for translating AI insights into practical, real-world actions.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 196 0 R
/E (AI systems rarely remain static. They often require feedback loops where outputs are monitored, errors are corrected, and new data is introduced. This ensures the system stays relevant and accurate over time, especially in )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
