394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (Agentic AI systems are frameworks of smart software programmes \(known as AI agents\) that interact to achieve specific goals. The defining feature of an Agentic AI system is its ability to make decisions within complex environments and act independently upon those decisions.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (Agentic AI systems have existed for some time, but their use has expanded with the development of Generative AI, particularly Large Language Models \(LLMs\) and diffusion models. Today, the use of Agentic AI systems, which are able to execute multi-step tasks with minimal human intervention, is being explored in various spaces, including human resources, finance and cybersecurity. As these systems continue to evolve, they could profoundly impact business operations, workforce dynamics, and strategic planning.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (The autonomous nature of Agentic AI systems adds additional layers of risk that should be considered in their deployment and use. These can include amplification of existing AI system risks, such as hallucination, memory poisoning and function misuse. As these systems become more capable of taking multi-step actions with less human intervention, organisations should ensure that clear oversight, accountability, operational boundaries and escalation mechanisms are established across the system lifecycle. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
