<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (The implementation of AI systems within businesses should not be approached as a simple plug-and-play exercise. Many organisations begin with enthusiasm but quickly discover that success requires more than acquiring a model or hiring a vendor. It requires a structured process that strikes a balance between innovation and governance.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (AI systems sit at the intersection of technology, people, and purpose. Without clear alignment to organisational needs, they risk becoming costly experiments with little value. Without strong governance, they can produce outcomes that are biased, opaque, or even harmful. Without proper readiness, they may never move past pilot projects into real deployment.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (To address these challenges, a disciplined framework for implementation is needed. This may include the following five \(5\) key stages. Each stage supports the adoption of AI that is purposeful, well-governed, trustworthy, and aligned with organisational objectives. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
