<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 208 0 R
/E (In addition to generally applicable laws, boards should identify and observe the sector-specific regulatory and administrative instruments relevant to their organisation and its AI use cases. The application and effect of each instrument will depend on the issuing authority, the organisations within its scope and the framework under which it is issued. A relevant example is the Guidelines on Technology Risk Management for capital market entities regulated by the Securities Commission Malaysia.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 208 0 R
/E (AI governance should not be treated as a standalone technology workstream. As AI can affect the environmental footprint, social outcomes and governance expectations, boards should integrate AI considerations into existing ESG and sustainability frameworks, including Enterprise Risk Management \(ERM\), internal controls and assurance processes, and stakeholder disclosures.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 208 0 R
/E (Accordingly, where AI is adopted within the organisation, boards should ensure there is clear oversight and accountability for its use, and that material technology, data and AI-related risks are disclosed in a proportionate manner to stakeholders, without prejudicing commercially sensitive information.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
