/E (This approach may be supported through an internal AI risk assessment and management framework. The framework should not assess and manage risk based on the type of AI technology alone, but on the specific use case, including what the AI system does, whose data it uses, the level of human oversight, and the potential impact of error, misuse or unintended outcomes.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
563 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (Organisations are encouraged to adopt or adapt a risk assessment approach that is proportionate to their size, sector, AI maturity, business context and applicable legal or regulatory obligations. Where appropriate, organisations may refer to recognised standards and guidance, including ISO/IEC 42001 on AI management systems, ISO/IEC 23894 on AI risk management, and ISO 31000 on general risk management principles.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
564 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (The objective is to ensure that higher-impact AI use cases receive stronger governance, assurance and oversight, while lower-impact use cases remain subject to suitable but proportionate controls. Boards should therefore ensure that management has a clear process to identify AI risks, assess their likelihood and impact, determine appropriate controls, document key decisions, and review the risk profile over time.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
