<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Capacity building should extend beyond staff training to include board-level fluency. Directors need to be equipped to ask the right questions of management and challenge assumptions. Boards may also consider appointing AI ethics or technology advisory roles \(internal or external\) to provide independent oversight and expert input at the board level.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Build Organisational Capacity and Advisory Support)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
611 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Trust is sustained through openness. Boards should encourage management to communicate transparently with regulators, investors, customers, and employees on how AI is being used and governed. This extends beyond general disclosure to include clear transparency notices that explain the role of AI in decision-making and, where appropriate, provide opt-out options for individuals who do not wish to be subject to automated processes. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
612 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Effective governance also requires that organisations establish appeal and redress mechanisms so that impacted parties can challenge or seek remedies for adverse outcomes. Communication on these issues should be delivered in a manner that is easy to understand, avoiding technical jargon so that stakeholders can make informed choices. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
613 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (By taking these steps, boards help ensure that disclosure is not only proactive but also meaningful, reinforcing accountability and trust.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [6]
>>
