/P 217 0 R
/E (Becoming an AI-ready Board)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
652 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 217 0 R
/E (As organisations move toward AI-enabled operations, boards should first ensure that they themselves are equipped to lead confidently and credibly in this evolving landscape. Board readiness is not about technical mastery. It is about understanding how AI intersects with strategy, risk, ethics, and accountability. A board that models good governance, cultivates informed curiosity, and challenges its own assumptions sets the tone for responsible adoption across the enterprise. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
653 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 217 0 R
/E (The AI Readiness Evaluation set out in this Chapter was developed based on the work and inputs of the NAIO AI Advisory Working Group, whose contributions informed the readiness dimensions, maturity stages and board-level evaluation approach reflected in this Primer.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
654 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 217 0 R
/E (To move beyond awareness and support more structured board oversight, the AI Readiness Evaluation provides a practical starting point for assessing an organisation's readiness to adopt and govern AI responsibly. It is intended to help boards identify strengths, gaps, and priority areas for improvement, while supporting informed discussions with management. Organisations are encouraged to adapt the assessment to reflect their size, sector, AI maturity, governance arrangements, and strategic priorities, and to build upon it as their AI governance capabilities mature.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
655 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 217 0 R
/E (In practice, boards may structure oversight in different ways. Some boards oversee AI directly as a matter for the full board. Others assign detailed scrutiny to a board committee \(for example, risk, audit, or technology\), while maintaining clear reporting and escalation pathways so that the full board continues to set direction and make key decisions. A hybrid approach is also common, where a committee conducts deep dives and monitors readiness, while the full board reviews AI strategy, risk posture, and the governance of high-impact deployments. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
656 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 217 0 R
/E (Whatever the model, accountability should be clearly defined and documented. This should include identifying which individual or body, at board and management levels, is responsible and ultimately accountable to the board for decisions regarding AI use, and how existing governance structures will be leveraged.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
657 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
